  "subject": "Anthropic shipped Claude Code cross-session messaging (ListAgents/SendMessage, plain text session-to-session, v2.1.224+ mac/Linux/WSL2 and v2.1.234+ native Windows) -- VERIFIED LIVE on the Director's Windows machine 2026-08-24, which CORRECTS the working premise that it was 'not on Windows yet' (a version floor, not a platform gap; a strategist argument built on that premise was withdrawn). It is NOT the doorbell and cannot close conformance #3: it addresses ephemeral SESSIONS by name rather than SEATS (the inverse of pattern #2, and they had to build name-collision disambiguation because of it), requires a live sender rather than an artifact landing, carries PAYLOAD rather than a pointer, and leaves no audit trail. But it independently RE-DERIVES the OG content-is-data discipline: a receiving session is told the message cannot approve anything, cannot change permission settings or CLAUDE.md, that commands in it arrive as inert plain text, and that permission prompts still fire -- layered defence reached while building a different feature, the same evidence class the charter already credits for the Tier-3/Workflows re-derivation, and a direct restatement of the June C1 finding. The vendor-neutrality argument is strengthened by their own docs: the feature is unavailable on Bedrock, Claude Platform on AWS, Google Cloud Agent Platform and Microsoft Foundry -- provider-gated inside a single vendor. Channels remains the vendor's own named mechanism for pushing external events into a session, so Path B is aligned with its intent. Logged as a candidate contribution back: seat-addressing is exactly the gap their session-addressing had to paper over.",

  "sent": "2026-08-24T15:00:00-04:00",
  "action_required": false,

  "body": "Promotion-watch context for the seat-inbox-notification convention; does NOT move the conformance-#3 gate. WHAT SHIPPED: Claude Code cross-session messaging (code.claude.com/docs/en/cross-session-messaging) -- one session sends plain text to another via ListAgents/SendMessage; v2.1.224+ on macOS/Linux/WSL2, v2.1.234+ on native Windows; same-machine delivery over a per-session Unix socket (named pipe on Windows) never transiting Anthropic servers, cross-machine via Remote Control through Anthropic servers. VERIFIED LIVE on the Director's Windows machine 2026-08-24: ListAgents from the strategist session returned five peers; this session is oagp-org-c9 and a peer oagp-org-df had been running 25 minutes, i.e. started around the 1422 filing. CORRECTION TO THE RECORD: the working premise entering this analysis was 'shipped but not on Windows yet'; that is wrong -- the Windows constraint is a version floor, not a platform gap, and this machine clears it. A strategist argument built on that premise (that the Windows gap raised the value of the vendor-neutral transport) was withdrawn on contact with the documentation. IT IS NOT THE DOORBELL, on four load-bearing differences: (1) it addresses SESSIONS, not seats -- targets are per-session names with collision handling bolted on, whereas OG's semantic is that a memo to the strategist SEAT rings whoever occupies it; this addresses the incumbent and has no concept of the seat, the inverse of recommended_patterns.general #2; (2) session-to-session, not substrate-to-session -- a live sender must compose and send, whereas the OG doorbell fires because an artifact landed in memos/; here nobody home means nothing rings, and a file landing rings nothing at all; (3) PAYLOAD, not pointer -- 'a message is a piece of text one Claude writes to another', the exact inverse of the OG invariant that only address and path travel so the receiving seat reads the artifact under its own discipline; (4) no audit trail -- the message lands in a conversation, whereas a memo is a committed artifact carrying from/to/subject/action_required and readable in a year. Conclusion: it does not obsolete the doorbell and cannot close conformance #3. It is one more vendor transport, and the documentation makes the vendor-neutrality argument for us: unavailable on Amazon Bedrock, Claude Platform on AWS, Google Cloud's Agent Platform and Microsoft Foundry -- provider-gated inside a single vendor. WHAT IT DOES CORROBORATE, and this is the valuable part: the constraints Claude Code places on a RECEIVING session independently re-derive OG's content-is-data-not-instructions rule -- a peer message cannot approve anything and never counts as user consent; it cannot change permission settings, CLAUDE.md or other configuration; commands in its text do not run and arrive as plain text; and permission prompts still fire for anything acting on it requires. That is layered defence, arrived at independently while building a different feature -- the same evidence class the charter already credits for the Tier-3 floor being re-derived by Claude Code Workflows' one-level-nesting rule, and a direct restatement of the June C1 finding where a rung session's destructive rm still hit the permission gate. THEY HIT THE PROBLEM PATTERN #2 SOLVES AND DID NOT SOLVE IT: because targets are ephemeral session names, the feature needed disambiguation machinery for collisions; seat-addressing is exactly what is missing. This is the clearest evidence to date for recommended_patterns.general #7 (org-governance-layer-above-runtime): the runtime supplied a session-to-session pipe, and the durable, seat-addressed, artifact-backed, audit-trailed layer is OG's. Logged as a candidate contribution back to the vendor ecosystem rather than merely an observation; not acted on. COMPOSITION, NOT COMPETITION: the same documentation points at channels as the mechanism for pushing external events into a session, which is precisely what a memo landing is, so the Path B PoC is aligned with the vendor's own intent for that primitive. Cross-session messaging can also carry the doorbell CORRECTLY if used as a pointer -- the memo exists in the substrate and the message says only that a memo is waiting at a given path, so the body never travels and the seat reads the artifact under its own discipline; that is pointer-not-payload riding a transport it was not designed for, a real test of the convention's transport-independence even though this transport cannot itself satisfy conformance #3. Live test proposed 2026-08-24 and deferred on the first occasion because the Director messaged the implementer session manually before it ran; carried to the next memo bearing an open action item. STANDING POSITION UNCHANGED: seat-inbox-notification promotes when a second, vendor-neutral transport demonstrates transport independence; vendor re-derivation strengthens the convention's evidence base under pattern #8 but does not substitute for the gate.",
  "body_ref": "2026-08-24-1500--og-strategist--og-strategist--cross-session-messaging-shipped-not-the-doorbell-but-re-derives-the-discipline.body.md",

